# SSH config for the agent identity's git remotes, reached via core.sshCommand in
# ~/.claude/gitconfig. Nothing else uses it — plain `ssh` is untouched.
#
# It deliberately does NOT include ~/.ssh/config. Two attempts at including it safely
# both failed closed only by luck. Scoping the override to `Host github.com` protected
# a remote spelled literally that way and nothing else: an alias with
# `HostName github.com` is a different Host pattern, so it imported the personal
# identity and pushed as the human account. Pinning IdentityAgent for every host took
# the personal AGENT away, but not personal keys — `IdentityAgent` only chooses the
# socket, and `IdentitiesOnly yes` still permits an explicitly configured file
# identity, so an alias pointing at a private key on disk would still have
# authenticated as the human. Nor can it be repaired afterwards: an ssh option keeps
# the first value it is given, and `Match final` re-parses without clearing what the
# include already set, so nothing placed after the include can take an identity back
# off. There is no way to include routing while excluding identities, so the include
# is gone and the identity below is the only one this config can ever offer.
#
# The cost is that an ssh alias in ~/.ssh/config means nothing here: a git remote that
# relies on one for HostName, Port or ProxyJump does not resolve. That is a loud
# failure, and the fix is to add its Host block to THIS file — which is also the
# moment to decide whether an agent should reach that host at all. Nothing is lost
# that would have worked anyway: with the personal keys unreachable, such a remote
# could not have authenticated regardless.
Host github.com
	HostName github.com
	User git

Host *
	IdentityAgent ~/.claude/run/agent.sock
	IdentityFile ~/.claude/claude-auth.pub
	IdentitiesOnly yes
